cast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Connecticut's presidential race is an open contest with the incumbent term-limited, leaving both parties to nominate fresh standard-bearers. The state's eight-point Democratic partisan lean forms the core of the outlook, and with no candidate-specific polls, expert ratings, or fundraising figures available, the forecast rests almost entirely on that fundamental.\n\nRecent headlines center on the 2028 primary calendars and down-ballot Connecticut contests rather than the general election itself, offering little signal about the fall campaign. An eight-point Democratic edge with wide uncertainty places the race in Lean D territory, though the margin could tighten or widen considerably once nominees are set and polling begins. The national climate, which has New England Democrats voicing concern about election interference, remains a key variable for both parties.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8243, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.781, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.7754, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.7619, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.7738, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.7843, "margin": 8.0}]}